<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lobengula</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1845–<a href="/years/1894">1894</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lobengula was the second and last king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndebele-kingdom">Ndebele</a> kingdom in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a>, succeeding his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mzilikazi">Mzilikazi</a> in 1870 and ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulawayo">Bulawayo</a>. Pressed by concession-hunters, he signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudd-concession">Rudd Concession</a> in 1888, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cecil-rhodes">Cecil Rhodes</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-south-africa-company">British South Africa Company</a> used to occupy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mashonaland">Mashonaland</a>. The company defeated his forces in the First Matabele War of 1893, and he died while fleeing north in early 1894.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lobengula.jpg" alt="Lobengula" width="320" height="450" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Humansdorpie at English Wikipedia. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lobengula-image.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
